## 4.3 Electrochemical measurements
4.3 Electrochemical measurementsCV, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), and chronocoulometry are conducted on an electrochemical workstation (CH Instrument, CHI660E) with a three-electrode configuration. Polarization curves and hydro-dynamic voltammetry are conducted on a bipotentiostat (CH Instrument, CHI760D) and analytical rotator (PINE instrument) with a three-electrode configuration. Catalysts modified glassy carbon (GC, \(0.0707\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) electrode is used as the working electrode. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) filled with saturated KCl solution is used as reference electrode, and Pt wire is the counter electrode, respectively. Detailed electrode preparation protocol for electrochemical characterization are provided in the ESM.
## 4.4 Constant potential production of \(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\)
4.4 Constant potential production of \(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\)Constant potential electrolysis of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is conducted in a homemade H-type electrochemical cell. The cathodic and anodic compartment is separated by Nafion-117 membrane (Dupont). Both chambers were filled with \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) as the electrolyte solution. GC plate with an area of \(1.0\,\mathrm{cm}\times1.0\,\mathrm{cm}\) is used as working electrode to support the catalysts. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) filled with saturated KCl solution is used as the reference electrode while Pt foil with an area of \(2\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) is used as the counter electrode. Prior to electrolysis, the GC plate is polished sequentially by 1.0, 0.3, and \(0.05\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) alumina powder. For modification, \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) dispersed catalyst ink is drop-casted onto the glassy carbon plate and was dried under gentle \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow. The modified GC plate is connected to a copper wire and sealed with silicone glue to prevent it from exposure in the electrolyte. High purity \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \((99.99\%)\) is purged into the electrolyte for at least \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) to completely expel impurity gases. During electrolysis, potentials are kept at \(0.4\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. RHE) for \(6\,\mathrm{h}\). At certain time interval, \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the sample aliquots is taken from the cathode chamber and diluted by another \(900\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\,0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) sodium acetate solution. The concentration hydrogen peroxide is then determined using Merck peroxide test kit.
